description: Take a complex task end-to-end — sharpen, plan, research in parallel, gated execution
argument-hint: "[the goal]"
model: {{ research_model }}
effort: high
---
Deliver this end-to-end: $ARGUMENTS

Run the pipeline in order, and don't advance a phase until the previous one is solid. Scale it to
the goal: for a small, clear goal, collapse phases 1–3 into a one-paragraph brief and get approval.
{{ guidance }}

{% if inquire %}
## 0. Route the goal
Classify the goal before anything else, state the classification, and let me override it:
- **Build / change** — produce or modify something → continue with phases 1–6.
- **Understand** — learn how or why the code works, no change wanted yet → run the `/inquire` loop
  instead of this pipeline.
- **Mixed** — understand, then change → run a short `/inquire` orientation first, then continue with
  phase 1 using its ledger as context.
{% if brief %}- A brief from `/brief` (`.claude/briefs/<slug>.md`, with or without a leading `@`): Read it first, its
  `Target:` line is its route — `deliver` continues with phase 1; `inquire` is `/inquire`'s brief{% if workstreams %} and
  `workstreams` is `/workstreams`'{% endif %}: say so and stop.
  If no such file exists, say so (a brief written in a worktree stays in that worktree) and stop.
{% endif %}{% endif %}
## 1. Sharpen the goal
{% if intent %}If the goal names an intent from `/intent` — its intent file, an issue draft without one
(`.claude/intent/drafts/issue-*.md`), or an issue that links either (an intent file's `Issue:` line, a
draft's `<!-- Issue: … -->` line) — read it first and check its sign-off. An intent file needs
`Status: Accepted`, with every approver approved in its Sign-off section; an issue draft needs
`<!-- Status: Accepted -->`, with every approver ticked approved in its Needs from checklist{% if approvers %}
(this project's approvers: {{ approvers }}){% endif %}. If not, don't start — list who is still pending and
stop. If I insist, say plainly that starting now bypasses the approvers' sign-off, and start only after
I confirm explicitly. Then treat its approved plan as the brief: confirm with me what changed since,
and skip what it already settles in phases 1–3.
{% endif %}{% if brief %}If the goal is a brief from `/brief` (`.claude/briefs/<slug>.md`, with or without a leading `@`),
Read it first; its `Target:` line must name `deliver` (another target's brief belongs to that skill — say
so and stop). Treat it as my answers: its Goal, Scope, Success criteria (keep their slugs),
Constraints, Out of scope and Risks settle phases 1–2 and seed phase 3's task list, Scope line and
risk register; its Context is the skim already done (re-check a `file:line` that looks stale); its
Inferred items are mine unless I contest one. Ask only what its Open section leaves open, then
present the plan for approval as usual. `Status: draft` is usable — say it is unfinished. If no such
file exists, say so (a brief written in a worktree stays in that worktree) and stop.
{% endif %}Rewrite the goal into a crisp brief using the prompt-engineering technique: explicit role, task,
and success criteria; remove ambiguity.

## 2. Gather requirements
First skim the relevant code (read-only, in parallel) so every question is informed — never ask
what the code already answers. Then interview me one question at a time — 2–4 default options
(recommended first), only what resolves real ambiguity; infer the rest and say what you inferred.
Cover scope, constraints, success criteria, and out-of-scope.

## 3. Plan and get approval
Draft a numbered task list + intent + a **risk register** (every risk gets a mitigation task).
{% if scope_guard %}Give each success criterion a slug too (the project rules' **Naming what you refer back to**), and add a **Scope** line: what will change, what is out of scope, and any
refactor, rename or dependency change the goal needs. `{{ scope_guard }}` checks the change against the tasks, the
criteria and the Scope line in phase 5.
{% endif %}Present it and **invite push-back**; iterate until I approve. Do not execute yet.

## 4. Research in parallel (read-only)
Investigate concurrently where independent: fetch the relevant online HTML/PDF docs — from the
trusted documentation sites only (the project's **Web research** rule) — and study the local source. Run these in parallel — exploration is side-effect-free.
One thread is always the precedent sweep: {% if explorer %}the `{{ explorer }}` subagent{% else %}a built-in `Explore` subagent{% endif %} gets the approved plan
and lists the precedents the change must follow, by the project rule **Precedents**. Keep the list with the
plan; for a small goal keep the sweep short, but run it. If what you find — a precedent included — contradicts
the approved plan, go back to phase 3 and re-plan rather than pushing through — at most 2 re-plans;
after that, stop and present the conflict to me instead of looping.

## 5. Execute under the gates
{% if scope_guard %}Before the first task, note the base for the scope check below: run
`git status --porcelain=v2 --branch --untracked-files=all` — its `# branch.oid` line is the commit you start
from, and the files it lists are mine, already modified.
{% endif %}Work the approved plan. With each task, give {% if implementer_agent %}`{{ implementer_agent }}`{% else %}whoever makes the change{% endif %} the precedents that bear on it,
verbatim. For each decision or completed task, **state a `Confidence: NN%`** and
surface gated decisions for me to confirm; keep working a risk until its mitigation round is done.
Give each task at most 3 attempts: if the same approach fails twice, change it; on the third
failure, stop and report the blocker with evidence.
Do not perform high-impact external actions (deploys, `git push`/merge, cloud mutations) without my
explicit approval.
{% if scope_guard %}
**Scope check.** When the plan's tasks are done{% if adversary %}, and before the adversary check{% endif %},
have `{{ scope_guard }}` check the whole change against the plan in both directions — nothing planned missing, nothing
unplanned added — the scope loop in `.claude/rules/ocgen-team.md`:
{% if implementer_isolated %}- First merge `{{ implementer }}`'s worktree branch into the working branch, or it checks code without the change.
{% endif %}- Give it the goal and the approved plan verbatim (after a re-plan, the newest one; if phases 1–3 collapsed, the brief;
  if an approved intent replaced them, its plan and acceptance criteria), the decisions I confirmed, the base,
  and the files that were already modified.
- Cuts: revert each one yourself, exactly as it says, and write nothing new; then run the tests, and
  undo any revert that breaks one.
- `Verdict: INCOMPLETE`: send {% if implementer %}`{{ implementer }}`{% else %}the subagent that made the change{% endif %} only the Partial and Missing items, verbatim, then have
  `{{ scope_guard }}` check the whole change once more. At most {{ scope_rounds }} completion round: an item still
  missing after it is **not done** in phase 6, never done.
- Differs (built another way than planned): don't rework it; show it to me in phase 6.
{% else %}
**Final check.** When the plan's tasks are done{% if adversary %}, and before the adversary check{% endif %}, check the
change against every success criterion in the approved plan (after a re-plan, the newest one; if phases 1–3
collapsed, the brief; if an approved intent replaced them, its acceptance criteria), and run the tests{% if worktree_implementer %} —
after merging `{{ worktree_implementer }}`'s worktree branch into the working branch, or you check code without the change{% endif %}. For a
criterion that isn't met or a test that fails, go back to the task that should meet it, fix it, and check again —
at most 2 rounds. A criterion still unmet after that is **not done** in phase 6, never done.
{% endif %}
**Conformance check.** After the {% if scope_guard %}scope{% else %}final{% endif %} check{% if adversary %}, and before the adversary check{% endif %}, {% if reviewer %}have `{{ reviewer }}`
check{% else %}check{% endif %} the whole change against the precedent list from phase 4{% if not reviewer %} yourself: the project has no reviewer{% endif %}.
{% if worktree_implementer %}- Merge `{{ worktree_implementer }}`'s worktree branch into the working branch before each check, or it checks code without the change.
{% endif %}- {% if reviewer %}Give it{% else %}Work from{% endif %} the goal, the approved plan, the precedent list verbatim, the base and the changed files. Each
  precedent is Followed, Broken or Not applicable, with its `file:line`. New code that duplicates an existing
  function, type or helper missing from the list is a finding too.
- A Broken precedent with a reason I approved is not a finding. Send {% if implementer_agent %}`{{ implementer_agent }}`{% else %}the subagent that made the change{% endif %} the other
  Broken items and the duplicates, verbatim, then check once more. At most one round.
- A precedent still broken after that is reported in phase 6 as broken, never as followed.
{% if privacy_guard %}
**Privacy check.** After the conformance check{% if adversary %}, and before the adversary check{% endif %},
have `{{ privacy_guard }}` check the whole change for personal or health data that could leak — every change, even one
that doesn't look like it touches personal data — the privacy loop in `.claude/rules/ocgen-team.md`:
{% if worktree_implementer %}- First merge `{{ worktree_implementer }}`'s worktree branch into the working branch, or it checks code without the change.
{% endif %}- Give it the goal, the base and the changed files. Never paste personal or health data into a prompt, a report
  or a commit message: name the field and its `file:line`.
- `Verdict: LEAK`: send {% if implementer_agent %}`{{ implementer_agent }}`{% else %}the subagent that made the change{% endif %} only its Critical and High findings, verbatim,
  then have `{{ privacy_guard }}` check the fixes. At most {{ privacy_rounds }} rework rounds.
- A Critical or High finding still open after that is **UNRESOLVED**: report it in phase 6 with its
  evidence, never as done.
{% endif %}{% if adversary %}
**Adversary check.** When the plan's tasks are done, and before phase 6, have `{{ adversary }}` check the whole change —
the adversary loop in `.claude/rules/ocgen-team.md`:
{% if implementer_isolated %}- First merge `{{ implementer }}`'s worktree branch into the working branch, or it checks code without the change.
{% endif %}- Give it the goal, the approved plan, the changed files and every agent's claims, quoted.
- `Verdict: REWORK`: send {% if implementer %}`{{ implementer }}`{% else %}the subagent that made the change{% endif %} only its Critical and High findings, verbatim,
  then have `{{ adversary }}` check the fixes. At most {{ rounds }} rework rounds.
- A Critical or High finding still open after that is **UNRESOLVED**: report it in phase 6 with its
  evidence, never as done.
{% endif %}
## 6. Synthesize
Summarize what was done, the decisions and their confidence, open risks, and next steps. Report
anything left unresolved — including every entry in `.claude/loop-guard/escalations.md` — as
unresolved, not done. If a coordinator session is named, report status to it with `SendMessage`.
From the conformance check, report each precedent followed, each one broken with its reason or as still broken,
and each duplicate found.
{% if scope_guard %}From the scope check, report the plan coverage (anything still missing, as not done), the blast radius,
every cut (reverted, or kept with its reason), every Differs for me to accept or reject, and the Defer list as
follow-ups.
{% endif %}{% if privacy_guard %}From the privacy check, report its verdict, the rounds used and every finding still open as unresolved;
put its Medium and Low findings under open risks.
{% endif %}{% if intent %}
If the goal came from an intent, draft the pull request that delivers it as
`.claude/intent/drafts/pr-<the intent's draft name>.md` (the intent's issue draft is
`.claude/intent/drafts/<the intent's draft name>.md`), by the project rule **Every pull request and issue
you draft needs the approvers' sign-off**: what changed and how it was checked, `Closes #<number>` from the
intent's issue link, a link to its intent file, and each approver's recorded sign-off on the intent, with
its link — then the **Needs from** section, one pending line per approver for their review of this pull
request{% if approvers %} ({{ approvers }}){% endif %}. Their approval of the intent doesn't approve the change: they review the pull
request too. Give me the `gh pr create … --body-file` command for it; I open the pull request.
{% endif %}
For several independent workstreams at once, run one `claude --worktree <name> --name <name>` session
per workstream (each running `/deliver`) and coordinate via `/list-agents` + `SendMessage` — see the
"Multi-session delivery" rule{% if workstreams %} — or let `/workstreams` manage them: it keeps the board, starts the
sessions where it can, and merges each branch once it is checked{% endif %}.
